  "cells": [
    {
      "id": "3ded4aef-04b5-4f46-8e8c-33aea44e4ed4",
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": "## Java 11 Changes\n\n### Language\n\n1. API changes\n    - String new methods\n    - Optional\n    - HTTP Client\n    - NIO Files\n    - Collection-Array Bridge methods\n    - `Predicate.not`\n    - IO & Path\n    - Some other Util API changes\n\n### Platform\n\n1. Run a single .java file directly with java.\n    - No explicit javac compilation step. \n    - The JVM compiles and runs it in memory. Ideal for scripts, prototypes, and education.\n\n2. Java Flight Recorder (JFR)\n\n    - JFR was a commercial Oracle JDK feature. \n    - Java 11 opens it to all. A low-overhead profiling and event-streaming tool built into the JVM. Now part of OpenJDK at no cost.\n\n```sh\n# Start JFR recording at launch\njava -XX:StartFlightRecording=filename=myrecording.jfr,\\\n         duration=60s,\\\n         settings=profile \\\n     com.example.App\n\n# Or start after JVM is running (jcmd)\njcmd <pid> JFR.start name=recording filename=app.jfr duration=120s\njcmd <pid> JFR.dump filename=snapshot.jfr\njcmd <pid> JFR.stop name=recording\n\n# Settings profiles:\n# default  — low overhead (<1%), suitable for production always-on\n# profile  — higher detail, suitable for short profiling sessions\n\n# Analyse with JMC (Java Mission Control)\njmc app.jfr\n```\n3. NEST based Access\n\n    - Fixes a long-standing inconsistency: nested classes (inner, static, anonymous) should have access to each other's private members without the compiler generating synthetic bridge methods.\n    - Java 11 formalises nestmates at the JVM level.\n\n4. Dynamic Class - File Constants\n\n    - A new JVM constant pool form CONSTANT_Dynamic that allows constants to be computed lazily at runtime via a bootstrap method.\n    - Enables language designers and bytecode engineers to create richer constant types cheaply.\n    \n5. Improved Unicode support\n\n    Java 11 fully supports Unicode 10.0\n    +8,518 characters, +56 new emoji, new scripts\n\n    New emoji in Unicode 10 — now representable\n    String pretzel    = \"\\uD83E\\uDD68\"; // 🥨\n    String broccoli   = \"\\uD83E\\uDD66\"; // 🥦\n    String dinosaur   = \"\\uD83E\\uDD95\"; // 🦕\n\n    // Character.isAlphabetic() / isDigit() updated for new codepoints\n    Character.isAlphabetic(0x1F1E6); // false — flag emoji letter\n\n    // New scripts: Masaram Gondi, Nüshu, Soyombo, etc.\n\n    // Charset improvements:\n    // New StandardCharsets constants:\n    StandardCharsets.UTF_8.name();     // \"UTF-8\"\n    StandardCharsets.US_ASCII.name();  // \"US-ASCII\"\n    // Java 11 makes more methods accept Charset directly (vs String name)\n\n    // Locale improvements:\n    // CLDR data updated — more accurate locale formatting\n    NumberFormat fmt = NumberFormat.getInstance(Locale.GERMANY);\n    fmt.format(1234567.89); // \"1.234.567,89\"\n\n    DateTimeFormatter dtf = DateTimeFormatter\n        .ofLocalizedDate(FormatStyle.FULL)\n        .withLocale(Locale.FRANCE);\n    LocalDate.now().format(dtf); // e.g. \"lundi 15 avril 2024\"\n\n\n## Experimental\n\n1. New GC _(Zero GC)_\n    - A new, experimental, scalable low-latency garbage collector. \n    - Designed to handle multi-terabyte heaps with GC pause times under 10ms regardless of heap size.\n    - Available on Linux/x86-64 only in Java 11 (Windows/macOS later).\n```sh\n        # Enable ZGC\njava -XX:+UnlockExperimentalVMOptions \\\n     -XX:+UseZGC \\\n     -Xmx16g \\\n     com.example.App\n\n# ZGC key tuning flags\njava -XX:+UseZGC \\\n     -XX:+UnlockExperimentalVMOptions \\\n     -XX:SoftMaxHeapSize=4g \\          # soft heap size limit\n     -XX:ZCollectionInterval=5 \\       # collect every 5 seconds (0=adaptive)\n     -XX:ZFragmentationLimit=25 \\      # max heap fragmentation %\n     -Xlog:gc*:file=zgc.log \\          # GC log\n     com.example.App\n\n# ZGC properties:\n# ✓ Concurrent — most GC work runs alongside application threads\n# ✓ Pause times <10ms regardless of heap size (even 1TB)\n# ✓ No generational (Java 11) — becomes generational in Java 21\n# ✓ Uses coloured pointers + load barriers (64-bit only)\n# ✓ Supports 8MB to 16TB heap\n# ✗ Slightly lower throughput than G1 (~15%) — tradeoff for low latency\n\n# Compare GC options in Java 11:\n# G1   — default, balanced (good for most apps)\n# ZGC  — experimental, ultra-low latency (large heaps, real-time)\n# Epsilon — experimental, no-op GC (see Epsilon tab)\n```\n\n2. Epsilon GC \n\n- A GC that allocates memory but never collects it. \n- When the heap is exhausted, the JVM exits. \n- Useful for performance testing, short-lived processes, and latency-sensitive benchmarks.\n\n```sh\n# Enable Epsilon GC\njava -XX:+UnlockExperimentalVMOptions \\\n     -XX:+UseEpsilonGC \\\n     com.example.App\n\n# With a fixed small heap (will OOM and exit after allocation exhausted)\njava -XX:+UnlockExperimentalVMOptions \\\n     -XX:+UseEpsilonGC \\\n     -Xmx256m \\\n     com.example.ShortLivedApp\n\n// Epsilon GC properties:\n// ✓ Zero GC pause overhead — ideal for benchmarking\n// ✓ Zero GC CPU overhead — all CPU for your app\n// ✓ Heap fragmentation stats available\n// ✗ No garbage collection — OOM if heap fills\n// ✗ Not for long-running production apps\n\n// Primary use cases:\n// 1. Performance testing without GC interference\n//    → Isolate allocation cost from GC cost\n// 2. Memory pressure testing\n//    → Verify your app fits in a given heap\n// 3. Very short-lived apps / CLI tools\n//    → Process that runs for <1s, exits before GC needed\n// 4. Latency benchmarks\n//    → Eliminate GC pauses from latency measurements\n\n// Example: verify no GC happens during a benchmark\n// If Epsilon runs out of heap → benchmark is not GC-neutral\n// → investigate memory allocations\n\n```\n\n## Removed\n\n- Java 11 removed several APIs that were deprecated in Java 9, and deprecated more for future removal. \n- This is the largest removal since Java 8.\n\n1. Module Removals\n\n```sh\nThese modules were deprecated in Java 9 and REMOVED in Java 11:\n\n✗ java.xml.bind    — JAXB (XML binding)\n✗ java.xml.ws      — JAX-WS (SOAP web services)\n✗ java.xml.ws.annotation — javax.annotation\n✗ java.corba       — CORBA\n✗ java.transaction — JTA\n✗ java.activation  — JavaBeans Activation Framework\n✗ java.se.ee       — aggregator module\n\nMigration — add as Maven dependencies:\nJAXB (XML binding):\n<dependency>\n  <groupId>jakarta.xml.bind</groupId>\n  <artifactId>jakarta.xml.bind-api</artifactId>\n  <version>3.0.1</version>\n</dependency>\n<dependency>\n  <groupId>com.sun.xml.bind</groupId>\n  <artifactId>jaxb-impl</artifactId>\n</dependency>\njavax.annotation (now jakarta.annotation):\n<dependency>\n  <groupId>jakarta.annotation</groupId>\n  <artifactId>jakarta.annotation-api</artifactId>\n</dependency>\n    Common compile error after Java 11 migration:\n    \"package javax.xml.bind does not exist\"\n    → Add jakarta.xml.bind-api dependency\n```\n\n2. Applet and related tools\n\n✗ Java Web Start — removed (JEP 289, deprecated Java 9)\n✗ Applet API    — deprecated for removal (final removal Java 17)\n✗ javaws tool   — removed with Web Start\n✗ Pack200 compression tools — deprecated (removed Java 14)\n\n3. Thread API removal\n    - Thread methods formally removed:\n        ✗ Thread.destroy()    — removed\n        ✗ Thread.stop(Throwable) — removed\n\n    ```java\n        // Use Thread.interrupt() pattern instead:\n        class SafeThread extends Thread {\n            public void run() {\n                while (!isInterrupted()) {\n                    doWork();\n                }\n                cleanup();\n            }\n        }\n        SafeThread t = new SafeThread();\n        t.start();\n        t.interrupt(); // safe cooperative stop\n    ```\n\n## Deprecated\n\n1. Nashorn Engine removal\n\n    // Nashorn JavaScript engine — deprecated in Java 11\n    // (Removed in Java 15)\n    @Deprecated(since = \"11\", forRemoval = true)\n    // ScriptEngine engine = new ScriptEngineManager().getEngineByName(\"nashorn\");\n    // Replace with GraalVM JS or other scripting engine\n\n    // Security Manager deprecated eventually\n    // (Formally deprecated in Java 17, removed in Java 24)\n\n    // Finalize methods on streams — deprecated\n    // FileInputStream.finalize() → use try-with-resources\n    // FileOutputStream.finalize() → use try-with-resources\n    // ZipFile.finalize() → use try-with-resources\n    // Use this pattern:\n    try (var fis = new FileInputStream(\"file.txt\")) {\n        // reads...\n    } // closed automatically — no finalize needed",
      "metadata": {
        "language": "markdown",
        "id": "3ded4aef-04b5-4f46-8e8c-33aea44e4ed4"
      }
